      "updated": 1786033786091,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "What is JAR?\n===================\n\nJAR stands for Java ARchive.\nA JAR file is a package file that contains:\nMultiple .class files\nResource files such as XML, properties files, images, etc.\nMetadata (information about the files), such as the MANIFEST.MF\nfile.\n\n\nWhy do we use JAR files?\n===========================\n\nThey help organize many files into a single file.\nThey make applications and libraries easy to share and reuse.\nThey are used to distribute libraries and frameworks that can be\nadded to Java projects.\nThe Spring Framework is also distributed as a collection of JAR\nfiles.\n\n\n\nJAR Files in Spring\n==================\n\nTraditional Spring Framework\nTo develop a Spring application, we had to download multiple Spring\nJAR files (such as spring-core, spring-beans, spring-context,\ncommons-logging,   etc.) and add them manually to the project's\nclasspath.\nSpring Boot\nIn Spring Boot, we do not need to download these JAR files\nmanually.\nWe simply add the required dependencies (usually using Maven or\nGradle).\nMaven/Gradle automatically downloads all the required JAR files\nfrom online repositories.\nSpring Boot's auto-configuration feature automatically configures\nmany components, reducing manual setup.\n\nImportant:\nSpring Boot does not download JARs because of auto-\nconfiguration. These are two different things:\n\nMaven/Gradle downloads the required JAR files.\nSpring Boot Auto-Configuration automatically configures the\napplication using those JARs.\nEasy way to remember\nJAR = A package that stores Java classes and resources.\nMaven/Gradle = Downloads the required JAR files.\nSpring Boot Auto-Configuration = Automatically configures the\napplication using those JAR files.\n\n\n\n*** Maven Repository is a centralized repository that stores JAR\nfiles and other project dependencies for various technologies,\nmaking it the most widely used and trusted source for downloading\nlibraries.\n\n\n\n\nAs per industry good practice there must be minimum three\npackages in our spring project:\n\n1. bean --> java class for spring context\n2. App --> Driver class\n3. resources --> configuration file",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "What is JAR?\n===================\n\nJAR stands for Java ARchive.\nA JAR file is a package file that contains:\nMultiple .class files\nResource files such as XML, properties files, images, etc.\nMetadata (information about the files), such as the MANIFEST.MF file.\n\n\nWhy do we use JAR files?\n===========================\n\nThey help organize many files into a single file.\nThey make applications and libraries easy to share and reuse.\nThey are used to distribute libraries and frameworks that can be added to Java projects.\nThe Spring Framework is also distributed as a collection of JAR files.\n\n\n\nJAR Files in Spring\n==================\n\nTraditional Spring Framework\nTo develop a Spring application, we had to download multiple Spring JAR files (such as spring-core, spring-beans, spring-context, commons-logging,   etc.) and add them manually to the project's classpath.\nSpring Boot\nIn Spring Boot, we do not need to download these JAR files manually.\nWe simply add the required dependencies (usually using Maven or Gradle).\nMaven/Gradle automatically downloads all the required JAR files from online repositories.\nSpring Boot's auto-configuration feature automatically configures many components, reducing manual setup.\n\nImportant:\nSpring Boot does not download JARs because of auto-configuration. These are two different things:\n\nMaven/Gradle downloads the required JAR files.\nSpring Boot Auto-Configuration automatically configures the application using those JARs.\nEasy way to remember\nJAR = A package that stores Java classes and resources.\nMaven/Gradle = Downloads the required JAR files.\nSpring Boot Auto-Configuration = Automatically configures the application using those JAR files.\n\n\n\n*** Maven Repository is a centralized repository that stores JAR files and other project dependencies for various technologies, making it the most widely used and trusted source for downloading libraries.\n\n\n\n\nAs per industry good practice there must be minimum three packages in our spring project:\n\n1. bean --> java class for spring context\n2. App --> Driver class\n3. resources --> configuration file",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    }
  ],
